from authoring import finish,ref,step,paragraph
finish('fully-dynamic-near-optimal-matching-polylog-updates',
 id='TCS-6627',title='Fully dynamic near-optimal matching with polylogarithmic updates',area='Dynamic graph algorithms',criterion='resources',question_type='yes_no',
 formal='For every fixed rational ε∈(0,1), is there a randomized fully dynamic algorithm that maintains an explicit matching M with |M|≥ν(G)/(1+ε) in every current general graph G using polylogarithmic expected amortized update time against an oblivious adversary?',
 definitions='The graph is simple, undirected and unweighted, on the fixed vertex set [n], n≥2; ν(G) is its maximum matching cardinality. It starts empty. An update inserts one absent edge or deletes one present edge. Updates arrive online, but the entire valid sequence is fixed independently of the algorithm’s random bits and is not revealed in advance. More precisely, for every fixed ε and constant a≥1, require constants K,d,B and a uniform randomized word-RAM algorithm A_{ε,a} such that every sequence of T≤n^a updates has expected total initialization-plus-update cost at most K[n+T(log₂(n+2))^d]. Words have B⌈log₂(n+2)⌉ bits; standard word operations, memory access and retained random bits are charged. With probability at least 1−n^{−3}, after every update in the sequence an explicit mate array represents a valid matching satisfying the approximation bound. Reading a vertex’s mate or the matching cardinality takes O(1) time; enumerating all matching edges costs O(n) and need not occur after every update. Changes to the mate array count toward update cost. Constants may depend on ε and a, but not on n, T or the sequence. No polynomial dependence on 1/ε, adaptive-adversary guarantee or worst-case per-update bound is required.',
 answer_criterion='An affirmative answer establishes all stated guarantees for every fixed ε. A negative answer rules out such algorithms for at least one fixed positive ε in this model, or provides a clearly identified conditional obstruction. Maintaining only an estimate of ν(G), maintaining a maximal matching, or allowing polynomial rather than polylogarithmic update time does not meet the target. Bounds requiring ε to shrink with n do not refute this fixed-accuracy formulation.',
 context=[
 paragraph('A maximum matching allocates the largest possible number of disjoint pairs. When an edge changes, recomputing it from scratch ignores the substantial information retained from the preceding graph. The dynamic problem asks how much of that information can be reused while keeping a solution within an arbitrarily small fixed relative loss of optimum. Both insertions and deletions are allowed, so previously useful structure can disappear.'),
 paragraph('The graph’s optimum changes by at most one after a single edge update, but a particular solution may require coordinated replacements. On a long alternating path, gaining one additional matched edge can involve changing many pairs. Approximation creates room to postpone or distribute some repairs. The difficulty is to do so without accumulating a large loss or spending too much time discovering the repairs that are needed.'),
 paragraph('An explicit solution is part of the target. A number close to ν(G) need not identify even one edge that can safely be used with the others. A mate array makes this distinction observable: a query at a vertex must return its current partner or report that it is unmatched. Requiring the whole array to be printed after every update would impose an artificial linear output cost, so the formulation instead charges only actual changes and queries.'),
 paragraph('Maximality is also weaker than near-optimality. In a four-vertex path, selecting only the middle edge gives a maximal matching of size one, while the two outside edges give a maximum matching of size two. Randomized maximal-matching algorithms already achieve very small update times against oblivious sequences. Chuzhoy–Khanna–Song’s STOC 2026 result improves deterministic maximal matching to n^{1/2+o(1)} amortized time; its guarantee still concerns maximality.','maximal'),
 paragraph('A separate sequence of breakthroughs beats the factor-two barrier for estimating matching size in polylogarithmic update time. Azarmehr–Behnezhad–Roghani’s SODA 2024 result obtains an estimate corresponding to a 2−√2−η fraction of optimum in general graphs, for fixed η>0. The paper explicitly studies size estimation. Its title alone can therefore suggest a stronger maintenance result than the theorem actually supplies.','estimate'),
 paragraph('For maintaining actual near-optimal matchings, Assadi–Khanna–Kiss’s SODA 2025 work develops a connection to ordered Ruzsa–Szemerédi graphs. Such a graph has its edges partitioned into matchings, with each matching induced in the union of itself and later matchings. Dense examples quantify how often certain expensive matching searches can recur. Their update bound is expressed through this extremal quantity and works even against adaptive adversaries.','ors'),
 paragraph('The dependence is roughly n^{o(1)} times the ordered-RS count for matchings of linear size, with accuracy-dependent parameters. A strong enough combinatorial upper bound would yield subpolynomial update time. This does not currently establish polylogarithmic time: n^{o(1)} can grow faster than every fixed power of log n, and the required extremal bounds are themselves unresolved. The connection is a route to algorithms, not a lower bound excluding other methods.','ors'),
 paragraph('Pratt’s SOSA 2026 note relates the ordered and ordinary RS notions at the level of polynomial exponents. Dense ordered examples would imply nearly comparably dense ordinary examples. It resolves a structural question about the reduction’s graph families, but does not determine the density needed for a polylogarithmic dynamic algorithm.','pratt'),
 paragraph('Liu’s 2024 work gives a different advance for bipartite graphs: amortized update time n/2^{Ω(√log n)} times a polynomial in 1/ε. It also relates stronger update bounds to online matrix-vector problems. The dependence on accuracy is essential in those statements. In particular, the ordinary-OMv equivalence stated for approximate vertex cover should not be silently transferred to matching, and a barrier using inverse-polynomial accuracy does not settle this card’s fixed-ε question.','liu'),
 paragraph('The adversary convention leaves useful flexibility. An oblivious sequence may be carefully chosen to be difficult, but it cannot react to random decisions or the current matching. The algorithm nevertheless receives updates one at a time; knowing that the sequence was fixed does not give access to future deletions. Similarly, amortization permits occasional expensive repairs as long as their expected total cost satisfies the bound.'),
 paragraph('The inherited Max Planck report explicitly identifies near-optimal fully dynamic matching with polylogarithmic updates as a long-term target. Its page 123 also distinguishes maintaining matching edges from estimating their number, and describes faster progress in the insertion-only setting. Allowing deletion of a vertex, which removes all its incident edges together, does not automatically supply an algorithm for arbitrary individual edge deletions.','primary'),
 paragraph('The fundamental issue is whether a changing combinatorial optimum can be tracked almost as cheaply as the graph itself is updated. A positive result would close a major gap between coarse approximation and near-optimal explicit solutions. It would also clarify how much global matching structure must be recomputed when each input change is local.')],
 why='The principal update-time frontier for maintaining actual near-optimal matchings under arbitrary edge insertions and deletions, beyond maximality and matching-size estimation.',
 importance=dict(score=95,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-11',reason='A central dynamic graph problem connecting approximation, explicit solution maintenance and extremal induced-matching structure.'),
 progress=[step('2023–2024','Polylogarithmic-update algorithms beat factor two for matching-size estimation; SODA 2024 extends the 2−√2−η fraction to general graphs.','estimate'),step('2024-03-05','Liu obtains n/2^{Ω(√log n)} update time for near-optimal bipartite matching, with polynomial accuracy dependence and connections to online matrix-vector problems.','liu'),step('2024–2025','Behnezhad–Ghafari introduce the ordered-RS route; Assadi–Khanna–Kiss improve its update bound to roughly n^{o(1)} times the relevant ORS count.','ors'),step('2025–2026','Pratt connects polynomial-density ordered and ordinary RS graphs; the result appears at SOSA 2026.','pratt'),step('2026-05-01','Chuzhoy–Khanna–Song post a deterministic n^{1/2+o(1)}-update maximal-matching algorithm, presented at STOC 2026. This is a weaker approximation target.','maximal')],
 references=[ref('primary','Sixteenth Biennial Scientific Report: March 2021–March 2023','Max Planck Institute for Informatics',2023,'https://pure.mpg.de/pubman/item/item_3527212_4/component/file_3527885/biennial-report-2023.pdf','Inherited reference; page 123, Dynamic Matching, in §27.5.1; PDF retrieved through the repository REST endpoint after the original download URL failed'),ref('estimate','Fully Dynamic Matching: (2−√2)-Approximation in Polylog Update Time','Amir Azarmehr; Soheil Behnezhad; Mohammad Roghani',2024,'https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/1.9781611977912.109','SODA 2024, 3040–3061; abstract explicitly specifies estimation of maximum-matching size'),ref('ors','Improved Bounds for Fully Dynamic Matching via Ordered Ruzsa-Szemeredi Graphs','Sepehr Assadi; Sanjeev Khanna; Peter Kiss',2025,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.13573','SODA 2025; v2 dated 18 October 2024; Definition 1.1, Result 1 and its parameter footnote, §1.1 on fixed ε versus shrinking ε'),ref('pratt','A note on Ordered Ruzsa-Szemerédi graphs','Kevin Pratt',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.02455','First posted 4 February 2025; SOSA 2026, 352–354, DOI 10.1137/1.9781611978964.27'),ref('liu','On Approximate Fully-Dynamic Matching and Online Matrix-Vector Multiplication','Yang P. Liu',2024,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.02582','Posted 5 March 2024; distinguishes matching, vertex cover, online and offline bounds, and polynomial dependence on inverse accuracy'),ref('maximal','A Faster Deterministic Algorithm for Fully Dynamic Maximal Matching','Julia Chuzhoy; Sanjeev Khanna; Junkai Song',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.00797','Posted 1 May 2026; STOC 2026, DOI 10.1145/3798129.3800868; maximal matching rather than a scheme approaching maximum cardinality')],
 status='source_open',status_note='Reviewed through 11 September 2026. No polylogarithmic-update algorithm meeting the fixed-accuracy, explicit-matching target was found. Matching-size estimators, ordered-RS-dependent bounds and the 2026 maximal-matching improvement do not resolve it.',formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-11')
